.TH tcldbg 1 9.1 Tcl "Tcl Applications"
.so man.macros
.BS
'\" Note:  do not modify the .SH NAME line immediately below!
.SH NAME
tcldbg \- Command-line debugger for Tcl scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtcldbg\fR \fIfileName\fR ?\fIarg arg ...\fR?
.BE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBTcldbg\fR is an interactive command-line debugger for Tcl scripts,
built on the debugger support commands introduced by TIP 86
(\fBinfo line\fR, \fBinfo return\fR, \fBtrace execution\fR and
\fBtrace breakpoint\fR).
It runs \fIfileName\fR in the same interpreter as the debugger itself,
stopping at breakpoints and single-stepping at command granularity.
Any \fIarg\fR arguments are passed to the script through the
\fBargv\fR variable, exactly as \fBtclsh\fR would.
.PP
On startup \fBtcldbg\fR presents its
.QW \fB(tcldbg)\fR
prompt without running the script, so that breakpoints may be set
first; the \fBrun\fR command then sources the script.
.SH "DEBUGGER COMMANDS"
.PP
Command names may be abbreviated to any unique prefix.  An empty input
line repeats the previous command.  Input that does not match a
debugger command is evaluated as a Tcl script in the frame in which the
debuggee is stopped, so ordinary Tcl such as
\fBset\fR \fIvar\fR or \fBinfo locals\fR works at the prompt.
.TP
\fBrun\fR
Source the script under the debugger.  Reports normal completion or an
uncaught error, then returns to the prompt.
.TP
\fBbreak\fR ?\fIfileName\fB:\fR?\fIline\fR (alias: \fBb\fR)
Set a breakpoint.  Without \fIfileName\fR the breakpoint is set in the
main script.  Breakpoints are numbered in order of creation.
.TP
\fBdelete\fR ?\fIid id ...\fR?
Delete the given breakpoints, or all of them if no \fIid\fR is given.
.TP
\fBdisable\fR \fIid\fR ?\fIid ...\fR?, \fBenable\fR \fIid\fR ?\fIid ...\fR?
Disable or re-enable breakpoints without deleting them.
.TP
\fBinfo breakpoints\fR
List all breakpoints with their state.
.TP
\fBcontinue\fR (alias: \fBc\fR)
Resume execution until the next breakpoint or the end of the script.
.TP
\fBstep\fR (alias: \fBs\fR)
Execute one command, descending into procedure calls.  At the initial
prompt, \fBstep\fR starts the script and stops at its first command.
.TP
\fBnext\fR (alias: \fBn\fR)
Execute one command, stepping over procedure calls.
.TP
\fBfinish\fR
Resume execution until the current procedure returns.
.TP
\fBwhere\fR, \fBbt\fR
Print the call stack of the stopped script.
.TP
\fBprint\fR \fIexpr\fR (alias: \fBp\fR)
Evaluate \fIexpr\fR with \fBexpr\fR in the stopped frame and print the
result.
.TP
\fBlist\fR ?\fIline\fR? (alias: \fBl\fR)
Show source around the current (or given) line; repeating continues
forward through the file.
.TP
\fBquit\fR (alias: \fBq\fR)
Exit the debugger, terminating the script if one is running.
End-of-file at the prompt is equivalent to \fBquit\fR.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.PP
The script runs in the debugger's interpreter, so procedures and global
variables persist across repeated \fBrun\fR commands.
.PP
Breakpoints and stepping rely on the source location information that
Tcl records for \fBsource\fRd files; dynamically evaluated code (for
example scripts built with \fBeval\fR) carries no file information and
cannot be stopped in.
.PP
If the script itself reads from standard input, its input and the
debugger prompt share the same channel.
.PP
\fBinfo return\fR reports the empty string when the previous command
was executed by bytecode-compiled code; see TIP 86.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
tclsh(1), info(n), trace(n), source(n)
.SH KEYWORDS
breakpoint, debugger, step, trace
